{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1467799-hedge-help-please","id":1467799,"title":"Hedge Help please","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-11-14T09:53:26-08:00","id":1467799,"title":"Hedge Help please","content":"So, due to a improbable series of events, it seems I will be running a Changeling game. Of course, my players seem to particularly interested in Hedge exploration, which means I'm going to need to know all the Hedge stuff backwards and forwards. Naturally I have some questions. Unfortunately I'm not sure they qualify as simple, but if they are I will move them to that thread.<br /> <br /> For reference, I only have Changeling 2e and Oak, Ash, and Thorn as my books but I'm trying to scavenge anything I can off of the internet. Thanks Satchel, the<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1424436-hedge-navigation-examples\"> hedge navigation examples </a>thread was very helpful.<br /> <br /> So, how unstable is the Hedge? If I shape the Hedge in a particular area, let say a forest to be a glacier, and none comes near that spot , will the glacier remain there? or will the hedge eventually go back to normal? Does the Hedge shape itself to match the seasons, at least in freehold territory?<br /> <br /> How out there can the Hedge get, environment wise? Like, can I have a section of the Hedge that's a fantasy space like Super Mario Galaxy, with a airy void with tiny worlds you must fly through or like the surface of the moon, cheese and all? If a trod goes through it, to a murky forest, is there in obvious boundaries or does it seem like the small forest planet your going toward, the more the world seems to expand around you until it seems to overtake the sky?<br /> <br /> So hedge navigation is dangerous, especially at low Wyrd and Clarity. If a Motley wanted to go exploring, what advice would a moderately helpful and cooperative freehold give, and what supplies would be recommended? There was a Goblin Fruit in 1e called Brumblebulb, apparently that granted instant exit from the Hedge. Should I have that available to them, or something like it?<br /> If it turns out the players really enjoy the Hedge, should I try to bring the Magi of the Gilded Thorn as a 2e style group entitlement?<br /> <br /> How many failed navigations should I say before they wind up back in Arcadia? I know you can make a Hedgeway going to The Hedge, but can you make one going back out into Ironside, or do you have to locate a exit?<br /> <br /> How &quot;safe&quot; are Hollows in and of themselves? Does the Hedge shape itself inside the Hollow? The Hegde Navigation thread had an example where someone was rolling to get back and forth inside his own Hollow, is that something I should do?<br /> <br /> Is it feasible to have the Freehold have a town Hollow inside of the hedge or is that too risky?<br /> <br /> Is a valid place in the Hedge a church with that offers Sanctuary for anyone and anything, as long they obey respect the Sanctuary of others. I was thinking of Hobs being the stained glass window people, able to move and show messages, but unable to speak.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>To whomever reads this, I hope you have a good day/night. May you be Happy.<br /> <br /> So, I made some Mage Legacies <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1497183-templebuilder-s-new-legacy-thread#post1497183\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>, with some help. They vary in quality, but I hope you take a look at them. Every one contains pieces of me, for better or worse.","edited":{"userid":26736,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","user":"TempleBuilder","date":". 11-15-2021, 12:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","name":"TempleBuilder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26736_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-14T20:32:40-08:00","id":1467854,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TempleBuilder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1467799-hedge-help-please#post1467799\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For reference, I only have Changeling 2e and Oak, Ash, and Thorn as my books but I'm trying to scavenge anything I can off of the internet. Thanks, the<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1424436-hedge-navigation-examples\"> hedge navigation examples </a>thread was very helpful.</div> </div> </div> </div>Glad it could help!<br /> <br /> (I <i>will</i> be getting back to that thing again sometime in the next couple of weeks, but I've been prepping for life stuff for the past several months. Will get to those mortal examples soon.)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, how unstable is the Hedge? If I shape the Hedge in a particular area, let say a forest to be a glacier, and none comes near that spot , will the glacier remain there? or will the hedge eventually go back to normal? Does the Hedge shape itself to match the seasons, at least in freehold territory? </div> </div> </div>If nobody shapes it away, the glacier will stay where it is. The Hedge may shape itself such that the glacier goes away, but the Hedge only shapes itself in response to stimuli and most places are more stable than the Thorns, i.e. a change made in a place where nobody goes very often will probably still be there when you come back to visit. (Unless you were followed, of course.)<br /> <br /> The Hedge is liable to shape itself to match the seasons largely in response to Seasonal courtiers being active in those areas; minor aesthetic changes are easy to come by as the Hedge reacts, and the Hedge shaping itself is (among other things) 2e's equivalent of the mechanics in 1e that established how far out and for how long other characters could track you in the Hedge by the psychoreactive shifts in the environment that followed you. The environment twists to reflect its inhabitants, especially the magically potent ones.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How out there can the Hedge get, environment wise? Like, can I have a section of the Hedge that's a fantasy space like Super Mario Galaxy, with a airy void with tiny worlds you must fly through or like the surface of the moon, cheese and all? If a trod goes through it, to a murky forest, is there in obvious boundaries or does it seem like the small forest planet your going toward, the more the world seems to expand around you until it seems to overtake the sky? </div> </div> </div>Generally speaking, the spaces of the Hedge are labyrinthine and obscured enough to hide the draw distance. Environments that don't resemble the surface of a planet are more the demesne of Arcadian follies, though there's little stopping you from coming across patches of Hedge where gravity doesn't work normally, especially in high-altitude regions or places out to sea and in the dark; spaces where you can routinely do the impossible are a bit of a stretch, but if you can reasonably see something fitting into the same space as Environmental Tilts it can probably be persistent part of the locale.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So hedge navigation is dangerous, especially at low Wyrd and Clarity. If a Motley wanted to go exploring, what advice would a moderately helpful and cooperative freehold give, and what supplies would be recommended? </div> </div> </div>Short. Trips. The place is built to isolate, trap, and enslave you, underneath the breadth of things you can track down in there, and the more you can take advantage of that second part by better matching the Old Gods in power, the more you can beat it away from the first part.<br /> <br /> Stick together. Have a guide, preferably one who maintains high Clarity and has a kith or entitlement bonus that lets them Hedgespin to help you out.<br /> <br /> Stay on the path. Take advantage of trods, move through freehold territory whenever you can, and know the route you're taking if it's not your first trip.<br /> <br /> Be prepared to compromise and/or spend resources \u2014 taking on Goblin Debt to speed your way or giving up on your initial destination if you get Lost are both real possibilities verging on inevitabilities if given enough time.<br /> <br /> Stay positive and don't rush. The Hedge is a predator and it can smell fear. Self-explanatory.<br /> <br /> Have a ride if you can get one. Magic horses and whatnot can generally outpace the Hedge, and less of you on foot means less footpaths to get tangled up in the Briars.<br /> <br /> Be specific in your goals and have a backup plan. It's less disastrous if you fail to navigate to the Blue Alley Trod and have to circle around to the Drawbridge Clearing Hedgeway than if you get lost and have to reorient yourself before you can find Any Exit At All.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There was a Goblin Fruit in 1e called Brumblebulb, apparently that granted instant exit from the Hedge. Should I have that available to them, or something like it? </div> </div> </div>Frankly, I'd just encourage them to buy Wyrd dots if they intend to mess around in the Hedge a lot. Being inconvenienced by personal oaths, token drawbacks, Bedlam, Hedge-borne paradigm shifts, and Clarity damage should all provide a ready supply of Beats to bootstrap their Magic Stat upwards.<br /> <br /> Alternately, take a cue from Dark Eras 2's Mysterious Frontiers writeup and have them <i>build</i> a freestanding doorway, if they're not too fussed about where they come out.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If it turns out the players really enjoy the Hedge, should I try to bring the Magi of the Gilded Thorn as a 2e style group entitlement? </div> </div> </div>It certainly couldn't hurt.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How many failed navigations should I say before they wind up back in Arcadia? I know you can make a Hedgeway going to The Hedge, but can you make one going back out into Ironside, or do you have to locate a exit? </div> </div> </div>You don't have to locate an active Hedgeway, but making a new one back to Earth will require you to find (or, again, build) a closeable portal you can fit through, and generally those portals look the same in the Hedge as they do outside of it (i.e. a wooden door is a wooden door, a manhole is a manhole, a tent flap is a tent flap, etc).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> How &quot;safe&quot; are Hollows in and of themselves? Does the Hedge shape itself inside the Hollow? The Hegde Navigation thread had an example where someone was rolling to get back and forth inside his own Hollow, is that something I should do? </div> </div> </div>Per the description of the Merit, a Hollow is by definition at least not in the Thorns and therefore, at worst, as plastic as the rest of the Hedge. Being inside one makes it harder to find out about you or track you down, which makes them safer for someone trying to avoid discovery in a place where finding you is as simple as succeeding at the process of finding you, but nothing about the inside of a Hollow intrinsically prevents you from getting hurt inside it.<br /> <br /> (For the record, the example of Hospitaller Bill involved a rush to get a patient from the front gate of his Hollow to a secure operating room; if he was just trying to get there to show off the place to a visitor it wouldn't have been a roll at all, but the same urgency that made that rush harder was what made the chance of failure interesting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is it feasible to have the Freehold have a town Hollow inside of the hedge or is that too risky? </div> </div> </div>Size Matters 2 is right there as far as scale goes, but the five-dot limit on Hollow means it may be infeasible as anything more than a general meeting space, especially given that benefiting from it requires a character to buy into it as a Motley Merit even if the &quot;extra&quot; dot does nothing.<br /> <br /> Practically speaking a large bit of Hedge infrastructure is more likely to be a series of Hollows connected through Route Zero and/or linked along one or more Stable Trods.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Is a valid place in the Hedge a church with that offers Sanctuary for anyone and anything, as long they obey respect the Sanctuary of others. I was thinking of Hobs being the stained glass window people, able to move and show messages, but unable to speak. </div> </div> </div>Aesthetically, yes, although I'd ask on what strength that offer is enforced and to what degree \u2014 hospitality oaths are generally an individual affair, and the most prominent place in which a general &quot;make no trouble&quot; agreement is in place is the average Goblin Market, so knowing what the catch is is paramount.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-15T08:14:58-08:00","id":1467882,"title":"","content":"Thank you for answering!<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-cat